A||N||20||Wildcat||The old engine swap question!||||1||
Z||000000||Wildcat||03-24-2001||03:01 AM||||I am sure it has been asked many times but I will ask again.  My son has an86 Fiero with a 2.8 litre and 4 speed.  Tranny, clutch flywheel are new, well used 3 weeks anyway. <BR>This car was owned by big brother first and has now gone through four 2.8's in the past five years. Every one the same problem, crankshaft shot. Once again this spring I will be installing a replacement engine.  If possible without spending a lot extra I would like to try the upgrade to a 3.1 or preferably a 3.4.  I consider these engines far superior to chevy's crappy early 2.8's!<BR>If I read correctly, intake and exhaust as well as tranny bolt right up to either of these, how about flywheel, electronics etc. <BR>I understand I must drill and tap new starter mounts also, which is not a problem.  I have many years experience in mechanics up to and including several restorations and engine/std tranny rebuilds as well as a well equipped shop. What years of 3.1's and 3.4's should I be looking at and from what make/model of donor car?  My opinion of these Fiero's is they look great but are a mechanics nightmare, tired of skinnin knuckles and want to make this the last engine swap for a few years.  Any and all suggestions appreciated!<BR>Thanks,<BR>Tim||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||03-24-2001||03:35 AM||||The really early 2.8's were known as "crank busters", but I haven't noticed Fieros having a big problem with breaking cranks.....I know I've never busted one.  They do have a tendancy to throw rods at higher miles.<P>At any rate, I would suggest a 3.4 over the 3.1......look for '93-'95 Firebirds or Camaros (they went to a 3800 in '96).  You will need to relocate the starter, but the Fiero intake, exhaust, etc. bolts right up as if it were a Fiero motor  originally.<P>If you do go with a 3.1 you will need a late 80's-early '90's Firebird/Camaro or GM minivans (they had the iron heads).  I would recommend the minivan motor since the starter is already on the proper side.  I think (but not sure) that the minivan motors have smaller valves than the Camaro or Fiero, so you may need to swap heads.<P>Hopefully this helps.....good luck.<P>BTW......do a search on this forum for 3.4 upgrades and you will pull up a bunch of good info.      ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||03-24-2001||03:35 AM||||Sorry......double post!<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 03-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||KRMFiero||03-24-2001||07:30 AM||||You might want to also look into the Caddy v-8 swap, its a little more work but its not hard <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx</A> <BR>also just search yahoo and other engines for Fiero and i'm sure you will find lots of sites that have done the 3.1/3.4 swap<P>Kyle<P>Welcome to the forum!||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||03-24-2001||08:46 AM||||Depending if you plan to rebuild the engine before installing, it might be cheaper foryou just to install a 3.1 crank in your existing 2.8 block. You will need compatible pistons though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wildcat||03-25-2001||01:57 AM||||Thanks for all the info and quick replies. I think I will go with the 3.4. I will be buying a rebuilt engine if I can find one. Hopefully I can convince the rebuilder to do the starter bolts so I don't have to worry about alignment. Only other question unanswered still, does the stock 2.8l flywheel fit the 3.4.  We have a nearly new one on the 2.8 and hate to waste it!<BR>BTW one rebuilder told me the 3.4 from a 93-95 camaro was fairly rare, is that so?<BR>Thanks again all.<BR>Tim||||reg||1
Z||000006||GarethWright||03-25-2001||03:26 AM||||please let me know how much the rebuilder wants to relocate the starter if its not too much hassle, as for the 3.4s being rare, Im not sure about that, but it seems we have quite a few of them being used by Forum members  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||03-25-2001||05:34 PM||||You need to use an '88 Flywheel.  I have a starter hole guide for rent if you need it, a forum member has it right now but I would be happy to put you next in line.  The rebuilder will need one.<P>fiero5sp@home.com <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierosound||03-25-2001||05:53 PM||||Wildcat...<P>Have the machine shop grind off the weight on the 2.8 flywheel, and have it rebalanced to neutral balance.  That's why I did  when I installed my 3.4L.  Later changed it to an aluminium flywheel anyway!<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-25-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000009||Tryxalon||03-25-2001||09:05 PM||||&lt;quote&gt; (on the 3.1) I would recommend the minivan motor since the starter is already on the proper side. &lt;/quote&gt; <P>I have an aluminum head 3.1 SFI V-6 from a 92 Cutlass my Niece wrecked that I'm prepping to replace the 2.5L 4-banger in mine when it does die. (4 cylinder is DAMN surprizingly durable) <P>Starter is ALREADY on the correct side (front of the motor if standing behind Fiero and transmission to the left).  The Minivan motor is a little easier to swap since it is 120 horse TBI setup and you can use the ECM and fuel pump from the 4-cylinder. <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000010||fyerstarter||03-25-2001||09:19 PM||||Sounds like a lot of work to me to go from 140 horse (stock v6) to 120 (minivan motor) :S<P>That's 1 step forward and two steps back.||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||03-26-2001||12:31 AM||||The stock 3.4-liter engine makes over 160 horsepower in a fiero and it also makes allot more torque. With a five-speed transmission you can get 0-60 mph times in about 6.5 seconds and run the quarter mile in about 14.8 seconds.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||03-26-2001||12:55 AM||||The reason to go with a minivan engine is because it has iron heads.  All the other FWD 3.4's use SFI and aluminum heads, which use different pistons.  You can't use aluminum heads with the Fiero intake, and you can't swap to iron heads without replacing the pistons.  welll.. you can, but your compression will be too low for anything other than a turbo setup.<P>I wonder if anyone's used the full 3400 setup out of the new grand am?  Aren't they running over 200 hp?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Tryxalon||03-26-2001||08:30 AM||||Ahh! thanks for pointing that out, Standard. (I did not know that!!) of course with the intake the horsepower would definately increase - but unless you are driving in highway conditions or in a drag race you won't use much horsepower. Torque, not horsepower is what you use most in "normal street driving" Unless you're Racing???!  <P>I'm actually planning to use the 3.1 intake with a K&N filter/snorkle and "make an airbox" to capture inlet and protect from heat/rain (as well as get it away from transmission cables and exhaust) but I can see where using the Fiero goodies would make it easier. (but I already have my engine - kinda committed!!!) <P> <p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||GT Bastard||03-26-2001||08:36 AM||||4 busted cranks in 5 years?  Somebody doesn't know how to build an engine, or the car is getting SEVERELY abused.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Carrolles||03-26-2001||09:16 AM||||Tryaxlon,<P>Take a look at this article before you spend a lot of time and money trying to make the intake system breathe better.  Apparently GM did a pretty good job designing the Fiero intake filter/water separator system.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Wildcat||03-26-2001||11:55 AM||||GT Bastard<BR>Crank one was in the car when it was purchased so I know no history on it except it went not long after car was purchased.<BR>Crank two rod bearing went after a year,threw rod out side of block. This motor saw abusive driving habits, cold starts, lead foot.  It was a low mileage used motor.<BR>Crank three, I purchased used motor that had low oil pressure, guess what rod bearings were badly worn, crank was ground by machine shop. I plastigaged bearings, sized and installed within specs. I would not say this motor was driven easy, but did not see enough abuse to justify rod bearings worn out after one year. <BR>Crank four, son purchased car that besides being an ugly junker had a motor that supposedly only had 30.000k on a rebuild.<BR>It lasted 3 weeks and has thrown a bearing!<BR>A lot of work down the tubes.  I have done two of the swaps (last two) myself and do not look forward to another one.  The Fiero is a very time consuming car to swap engines in.<BR>I had this same engine in a Chevy S15 PU<BR>that at 8 years old was on its third 2.8 litre which was in poor shape.  I drove it on the final engine only, for four years, but was never impressed with the performance of the 2.8's performance at all. <BR>I prefer my Fiat Spiders, DOHC 1800cc twin wwweber carb, great mileage and the stock Fiat leaves any stock Fiero eatin dust!<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Standard||03-26-2001||01:14 PM||||Add lead-in grooves to the #2 and 3 main bearing journals on the crankshaft and the block's upper main bearing bores.  Or get an 87-up block and crankshaft, they have an improved oiling system.  Find the book "How to Rebuild Your GM V-6 60* Engine" or I can send you the info you need.||||reg||1
Z||000018||ThatRickGuy||03-26-2001||05:22 PM||||also, make sure you're not running too rich! I've seen a rich engine chew threw bran new berrings in under 100 miles. of course that was a 350 hopped up w/ and ubsurdly huge carb sitting on top. but it could still be the problem. and make sure the drivers are checking the oil, if it's a little low, it's not hard to starve the engine on a sharp corner.||||reg||1
Z||000019||88formula||03-26-2001||08:03 PM||||93-95 Camaro 3.4-liter has iron heads. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Wildcat||03-26-2001||09:49 PM||||Thats the one I will be getting, thanks for all the info! Will be starting the project in the next month prob. and will let all know how it goes.  Son is getting anxious to get his car back instead of mom's 87 Taurus, and has nearly enough funds already.<P>Tim||||reg||1
